Construct a mathematically rigorous and physically consistent theory of quantum gravity. Concretely, give a well-defined mathematical framework (for instance a Hilbert space of states, a set of observables forming a suitable algebra, and a dynamical law) that:

(a) reduces, in an appropriate classical/low-energy limit, to Einstein's general relativity, in which spacetime is modeled by a smooth Lorentzian 4-manifold (M, g) whose metric g satisfies the Einstein field equations
   R_{μν} − (1/2) R g_{μν} + Λ g_{μν} = (8πG/c^4) T_{μν};

(b) reduces, in an appropriate flat-spacetime or weak-gravity limit, to standard quantum mechanics / quantum field theory, including unitary time evolution and the usual probabilistic interpretation of measurements;

(c) is internally mathematically coherent (free of the non-renormalizable ultraviolet divergences and other inconsistencies that plague the naive perturbative quantization of general relativity) in the regime where both quantum effects and strong gravitational effects are simultaneously relevant (e.g. near black-hole singularities and at the Planck scale);

(d) provides definite, in-principle computable predictions for physical quantities in this regime.

The problem is to either exhibit such a theory together with a proof of its consistency, or to prove that no such theory can exist within a specified set of axioms.

